This C program uses the `libgpiod` library to control two hobby servos connected to GPIO pins 4 and 5 on a Raspberry Pi. It reads four push-button inputs connected to GPIO pins 6, 7, 8, and 9, which control the movement of the servos. The servos are controlled using software PWM with a period of 50 Hz (20 ms) and pulse widths ranging from 1 to 2 ms, corresponding to duty values from 50 to 100 out of 1000.

The program initializes the GPIO pins, sets up the servos at the mid-position, and enters a main loop where it polls the buttons and adjusts the servo positions accordingly. It uses `SIGINT` signal handling to cleanly exit and release all resources when the program is interrupted.

Please ensure that you have `libgpiod` installed on your Raspberry Pi and that the GPIO pins used are correctly connected to the servos and buttons. The program assumes that the buttons are active-low with internal pull-ups enabled, which is the default configuration for Raspberry Pi GPIO pins.

