This C program uses the `pigpio` library to interact with the Raspberry Pi GPIO and I2C interfaces. It sets up GPIO2 as a falling-edge interrupt to trigger data logging from a BMP180 sensor connected over I2C bus 1 at address 0x77. The program uses oversampling setting OSS=3 for pressure readings and logs timestamped pressure and temperature data to `altitude_log.txt` in CSV format.

The program initializes `pigpio`, sets up the BMP180 on GPIO2 with a falling-edge interrupt, and opens an I2C bus. It then enters a main loop that runs indefinitely, polling the BMP180 at 1-second intervals, reading data, and logging it to the file. Upon receiving a SIGINT (Ctrl+C), the program cleans up resources and exits gracefully.

Make sure to compile the program with the `pigpio` library linked. For example, using GCC, you would compile it with:

```sh
gcc -o bmp180_logger bmp180_logger.c -lpigpio
```

Before running the program, ensure that the `pigpio` daemon is running on your Raspberry Pi. You can start it with:

```sh
sudo pigpiod
```

And remember to have the BMP180 sensor connected to the I2C bus 1 (GPIO2 for SDA and GPIO3 for SCL) and the VCC and GND pins properly connected.

