This C program uses the `pigpio` library to control GPIO pins on a Raspberry Pi 4 Model B. It implements a closed-loop flow control system for an ASCO RedHat 8210 series proportional solenoid valve, using GPIO 18 for PWM control and GPIO 23 for enabling/disabling the valve. It counts pulses from a YF-S201 flow sensor on GPIO 24 and adjusts the PWM duty cycle to maintain a target flow rate specified as a command-line argument.

The program initializes `pigpio`, sets up GPIO modes, registers an interrupt for pulse counting, and logs data to a local CSV file. It handles SIGINT and SIGTERM signals for graceful shutdown and resource release. The proportional controller adjusts the valve position based on the error between the target and current flow rates, clamping the valve position to the valid range [0, 255].

Ensure you have the `pigpio` library installed on your Raspberry Pi and compile the program with `gcc`:

```bash
gcc -o flow_control flow_control.c -lpigpio
```

Run the compiled program with a target flow rate argument:

```bash
./flow_control 5.0
```

This will start the flow control system with a target flow rate of 5.0 L/min. The program will log data to `flow_log.csv` and respond to SIGINT and SIGTERM signals for graceful shutdown.

